1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. Al-Ṣūfī saw — two skies.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
Around 964, the Persian astronomer
al-Ṣūfī presented his ruler with a book.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:21,000
He re-checked Ptolemy’s 48 Greek
constellations against the sky, star by
star.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
Under each Greek constellation, he added
the old Arab star names.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:31,000
Arcturus: “the raised one with a lance”.
Spica: “the unarmed raised one”.

6
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:37,000
He drew in some Arab constellations too:
a great fish across Andromeda.

7
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:43,000
By the fish’s mouth, he noted a little
smudge, like a cloud.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:50,000
It’s the Andromeda Galaxy, 2.5 million
light-years away — the earliest known
record of it.

9
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:59,000
Perseus carries a ghoul’s head — raʾs
al-ghūl in Arabic. Today we call the
star Algol.

10
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:06,000
After dark, Arcturus shines high, and
Spica lower to the south — the two
raised ones.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Later in the evening, look east: Vega
and Altair.

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:16,000
“The swooping eagle” and “the flying
eagle” — that’s where their names come
from.

13
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
Who decided the 88 constellations?

14
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

